For the second code, the `RemoveAt()` does not immediately modify the index as asked. It should use `index--` to avoid off-by-one issues at the `RemoveAt()` call itself.
<code>
public virtual void RemoveAt(int index) {
    for (int i = index; i < _size - 1; i++) {
        _items[i] = _items[i + 1];
    }
    _items[_size - 1] = null;
    _size--;
    _version++;
}
</code>
Replace with below:
<code>
public virtual void RemoveAt(int index) {
    for (int i = index; i < _size - 1; i++) {
        _items[i] = _items[i + 1];
    }
    _items[_size - 1] = null;
    _size--;
    index--;
    _version++;
}
</code>
And this will pass the assertion in the `RemoveAt()` method.
<code>
Debug.Assert(x = OLD(x) - 1);
</code>
But `x` is not decremented here. To decrement `x`, it should be:
<code>
x--;
Debug.Assert(x = OLD(x));
return x;
</code>
Here `OLD(x)` refers to the current value of x before the call to `RemoveAt()` and `RET` refers to the value of x after the call to `RemoveAt()`.--------------------
